# FILES THAT MUST NEVER REACH THE PUBLIC ORIGIN.
#
# Vercel serves anything in the deployment as a static asset. On 2026-08-25 a
# live probe confirmed four internal documents were fetchable at the domain
# root, one of which (CLAUDE.md, 19.9 KB) published the opaque slug of the
# private owner page. That slug is the ONLY access control on the page that
# carries the commercial inbox, with names and email addresses on it.
#
# Excluding them here stops them being uploaded at all, which is stronger than
# a redirect: a file that was never deployed cannot be served, cached, indexed,
# or recovered from a stale edge node.
#
# vercel.json also carries a 404 redirect for /*.md as defence in depth, so a
# markdown file added later is blocked even if this list is not updated.

# ---- internal documentation and working notes -------------------------------
*.md
!404.md

# ---- research corpus, never deployed by design ------------------------------
research/

# ---- enterprise diligence package, internal by nature ------------------------
# Added 2026-09-08 with the package itself. The *.md rule above already covers
# most of it, but the package carries a machine-readable preflight state as a
# .txt, which *.md does NOT catch and which vercel.json's /*.md redirect does
# not catch either. An internal working record at a guessable public path is the
# same exposure class as the 2026-08-25 finding recorded at the top of this file,
# so the directory is excluded whole rather than by file extension: a document
# added to this package later is then internal by default instead of by luck.
docs/enterprise-diligence/

# ---- unpublished article preparation, never deployed ------------------------
# Markdown only, so *.md above already covers it. Named explicitly as defence
# in depth: an outline for an unsubmitted piece at a guessable public path is
# the same exposure class as the 2026-08-25 finding recorded at the top.
cep-article-prep/

# ---- build, audit and one-shot maintenance scripts ---------------------------
# Not served by Vercel as functions, but they are static files under the
# deployment root and would otherwise be fetchable, and several name internal
# table structures, opaque endpoint slugs and study codes.
scripts/

# ---- build output, never deployed -------------------------------------------
# Added 2026-09-13 with scripts/generate_field_guides.py. The generator renders
# candidate PDFs for review; they are NOT the shipped guides and must never be
# reachable at a public path, or a reader could download an unreviewed rebuild
# from a guessable URL and take it for the real document. Same exposure class as
# the 2026-08-25 finding recorded at the top of this file.
build/

# ---- generated documents ----------------------------------------------------
*.docx

# ---- data extracts ----------------------------------------------------------
*.csv

# ---- Supabase edge function source, not a Vercel asset -----------------------
# Added 2026-09-15. VERIFIED ON PRODUCTION: /supabase/functions/run-study/index.ts
# returned 200. It is a Supabase Edge Function deployed by the Supabase CLI, so
# Vercel was serving its SOURCE as a static file: the study record corpus, the
# model identifier and the deployment instructions naming the secret names.
#
# The nightly cron in vercel.json calls /api/run-study, the Vercel function, not
# this one, so excluding it removes nothing that runs.
supabase/

# ---- database schema and policy definitions ---------------------------------
# Added 2026-09-15 after an adversarial pass found that only supabase-ALL.sql was
# excluded while TEN other root .sql files were not. VERIFIED ON PRODUCTION, not
# inferred: /supabase-engine-reviews-setup.sql and /supabase-setup.sql both
# returned 200, while the excluded /supabase-ALL.sql returned 404.
#
# supabase-engine-reviews-setup.sql publishes, at a guessable public URL, the
# exact anon SELECT grant that blocker B-013A treats as an open, undispositioned
# exposure, together with the full column list and a now-false retention comment.
# Seven further files carry "for select to anon using (true)", which between them
# map which tables are anonymously readable.
#
# This is the same exposure class the scripts/ rule above cites as its reason.
# The wildcard replaces the single-file rule so a schema file added later is
# internal by default rather than by luck.
*.sql

# ---- templates --------------------------------------------------------------
templates/

# ---- derived standard artifacts, NOT YET APPROVED FOR PUBLICATION ------------
# Added 2026-09-14 in Phase 1. standard/jrs-conditions.json is a machine-readable
# derivation of the five published Review Conditions. It is accurate and it is
# version-controlled, but Section 23 requires human approval before anything is
# published externally, and a machine-readable standard is a publication. It is
# excluded until that approval exists, then this rule is removed deliberately
# rather than by accident.
standard/

# ---- JRS control state, internal by nature -----------------------------------
# Added 2026-09-14 with the Enterprise Asset Orchestrator. These registries name
# contributors, rights positions, open security blockers and the opaque slugs of
# the private owner surfaces. A control file reachable at a guessable public path
# is the same exposure class as the 2026-08-25 finding at the top of this file.
.jrs/

# ---- manifest implementation, controlled by the public/private boundary ------
# Added 2026-09-15. The red-team pass flagged that lib/manifest/, tools/ and
# tests/manifest/ sit in a repository that deploys to a public host, and that
# nobody had checked whether they would be served. They would have been.
#
# VERIFIED RATHER THAN ASSUMED, on production, 2026-09-15: /openapi.json and
# /openapi-review-engine.json both return 200. A root .json that is not excluded
# here IS served, so a .js under lib/ or tools/ would have been too. The check
# used an existing served file rather than deploying anything to find out.
#
# The governing rule is PUBLICIZE THE STANDARD, PROTECT THE IMPLEMENTATION.
# Generator internals, the validator and the test harness are implementation.
lib/
tools/
tests/

# ---- independent review package, NOT YET APPROVED FOR PUBLICATION ------------
# Added 2026-09-15, immediately after creating it, because building the package
# COPIED tools/validate-manifest.js and schemas/schema.json into docs/, which
# would have republished at a servable path exactly what the two rules below
# had just excluded. The mistake was made and caught in the same cycle; the
# rule is written here so the next person does not have to rediscover it.
#
# This package is DESIGNED to be handed to a reviewer or a buyer. Handing it to
# someone is a deliberate act. Serving it at a guessable public URL is not.
docs/manifest-independent-review/

# ---- manifest schema, publishable candidate but NOT YET APPROVED -------------
# Excluded on exactly the reasoning already applied to standard/ above: a
# machine-readable schema is a publication, and Section 23 requires human
# approval before anything is published externally. The schema is a GOOD
# candidate for publication, which is why this rule is written to be removed
# deliberately rather than left to be forgotten. Removing it IS the act of
# publishing, and should be a recorded decision rather than a side effect.
schemas/

# ---- local tooling ----------------------------------------------------------
.claude/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
